      <abstract>
        <p>
          <bold>syn90001</bold>
        </p>
        <p><bold>Background: </bold>Here is the revised abstract: Circulating tumor DNA (ctDNA) may provide an early indication of treatment response in hormone receptor-positive metastatic breast cancer. We evaluated changes in plasma variant allele fraction during the first eight weeks of therapy. <bold>Methods: </bold>We prospectively enrolled 84 patients starting endocrine therapy plus a CDK4/6 inhibitor. Plasma was collected at baseline, week 2, and week 8. A prespecified molecular response was defined as a decrease of at least 50% in the dominant variant allele fraction. <bold>Results: </bold>Molecular response occurred in 57 patients (67.9%). Responders had longer median progression-free survival than nonresponders (18.4 versus 8.7 months). The association remained significant after adjustment for disease burden and prior therapy. <bold>Conclusions: </bold>Early ctDNA decline was associated with improved clinical outcome and warrants prospective validation. Research Sponsor: None. </p>
      </abstract>
